{{/* Skip secrets when the 1Password desktop app isn't running, so
     onepasswordRead doesn't abort apply. Gate on the app PROCESS, not on
     `op` itself: invoking op here would trigger the CLI<->app integration
     prompt (Touch ID) and then hang waiting on the absent desktop app.
     ignore = leave the existing rendered secrets.zsh untouched, preserving
     a previously-set key.

     Do not "improve" this into an `op whoami` check. Measured 2026-09-01:
     it answers a different question. whoami reports on a CLI session (the
     kind `op signin` creates), while onepasswordRead goes through the
     CLI<->app integration and biometrics, which creates no CLI session —
     so whoami printed "account is not signed in" at the same moment
     onepasswordRead was reading secrets fine. Gating on it would skip
     secrets.zsh on a perfectly healthy machine. `op account list` is no
     better: it answers from local config without touching the vault.

     What this guard still misses is the app running but locked, which
     ends in onepasswordRead's authorization timeout and aborts apply.
     That state is not cheaply detectable: any probe able to see it is the
     same call as the real read and hangs identically. Unlock 1Password
     before a full apply. */}}
{{ if ne (output "sh" "-c" "pgrep -xq 1Password && echo ok || true" | trim) "ok" }}
.config/zsh/secrets.zsh
{{ end }}
